{# The QUOTE's own controls in the shared action bar — the slot
   `admin/_partials/sales_document_action_bar.html.twig` renders after the fixed View / Edit / PDF
   set. Expects: `document` (the Estimate), `locked` (Accepted or Rejected) and `frozen` (an
   explicit document lock).

   Accept stays HERE, in the bar, as a primary button. The owner's Zoho reference puts Mark as
   Accepted and Mark as Declined in the overflow, which is a real observation and is in the report —
   but moving them is a UX decision he has not made, and accepting on the customer's behalf is one
   of the three blocks he has ruled on. #}
{% if document.convertedOrder %}
    <a id="quote-action-converted-order" class="button" href="{{ path('admin_order_detail', {id: document.convertedOrder.id}) }}">View Converted Order</a>
{% endif %}
{% if document.status == 'Priced' and not frozen %}
    {# Admin accepts on the customer's behalf — e.g. the customer called/emailed to say
       "proceed" and can't click their own Accept button. Only offered in Priced, same as the
       customer's own Accept control, and gated separately from Reject (below), which stays
       available more broadly (see the `locked` guard). See #392.

       Accepting creates nothing. The sales order is the separate Convert control below, and
       the invoice a third step on the order itself — so the confirm no longer promises an
       order, which it did while accept() was also the conversion.

       `not frozen` is the explicit lock: EstimateController::accept() calls
       assertWritable($estimate, 'accepted'), so on a frozen quote this button has one outcome. #}
    <form method="post" action="{{ path('admin_estimate_accept', {id: document.id}) }}" style="display:inline;" onsubmit="return confirm('Accept this quote on the customer\'s behalf? This cannot be undone.');">
        {{ csrf_field() }}
        <button id="quote-action-accept" class="button primary" type="submit">Accept Quote</button>
    </form>
{% endif %}
{% if document.status == 'Accepted' and not document.convertedOrder and not frozen %}
    {# Step two of three: Accepted quote → sales order. Hidden once an order exists, because
       the "View Converted Order" link above is what an already-converted quote offers and a
       second order must never be raised from one quote.

       "Convert", not "Create": the order screen's own billing control says "Convert to
       Invoice" and the link above says "View Converted Order", so this page and its
       neighbour already speak of turning one document into another.

       The confirm is UX only — a misclick mints a real document with a real order number.
       It is NOT the guard: admin_estimate_convert refuses anything that is not an Accepted
       quote with no order, and EstimateConversionService::claimForConversion() settles two
       admins pressing it at the same moment. A POST that never opened this page is refused
       by the same two — and by assertWritable($estimate, 'converted') when it is frozen,
       which is what `not frozen` here declines to invite anybody into.

       Deliberately the same plain-button-in-a-form shape as Accept above rather than a
       dropdown: every control in this part of the nav is a plain button, and a second sibling
       action later is one more form in this slot. #}
    <form method="post" action="{{ path('admin_estimate_convert', {id: document.id}) }}" style="display:inline;" onsubmit="return confirm('Create a sales order from this quote?');">
        {{ csrf_field() }}
        <button id="quote-action-convert" class="button primary" type="submit">Convert to Sales Order</button>
    </form>
{% endif %}
{% if not locked and not frozen %}
    {# admin_estimate_update_status calls assertWritable($estimate, 'changed'), so a frozen quote
       is refused a rejection exactly as it is refused an edit. #}
    <button id="quote-action-reject" type="button" class="button danger js-estimate-reject" data-url="{{ path('admin_estimate_update_status', {id: document.id}) }}">Reject Quote</button>
{% endif %}
